00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.150 align:start position:0%
grabbing things in space is tricky  unless those things are designed  specifically to fit together it can be a  bit hard to latch on with no atmosphere  suction is out and sticky adhesives are  rendered useless by extreme cold and  degrading radiation so scientists look  to nature for new ideas finding  inspiration in the toes of geckos who  easily scramble up even the smoothest  surfaces the key toes remarkable ability  are microscopic hair strategically  arranged on a geckos feet when pressed  these hairs flatten out against the  surface and tiny forces of molecular  attraction add up across the many points  of contact creating instant grip  scientists try to mimic this technique  by creating tiny wedges that act  similarly to these hairs forces that  smoosh the wedges into a surface create  grip while ten gentle forces peel the  material away by adding this material to  robotic contraptions scientists could  pick up and release objects remotely and  even direct a robot to walk across a  wall scientists tested the grippers on a  minimal friction platform to show that  robots in zero gravity should be able to  grasp objects like solar panels instead  of simply pushing them away  and during the nosedive induced  weightlessness of nasa's famous  zero-gravity aircraft training  astronauts use them to play catch with  both flat and curved objects the  researchers also sent miniature grippers  into space where they stuck to the walls  of the International Space Station  developing such a tool could be  important for many space projects from  clearing out floating debris orbiting  our planet to performing dangerous tasks  with robots  so that astronauts can stay safe  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]
 

